====================
Git Bash commands
=================

Note:

On Ubuntu we normally use the Linux terminal directly.

We do NOT need Git Bash.

Git commands work directly from the Ubuntu terminal.

git init : To initialize a Git repository

```
    git init
```

git status : To check working tree status

```
    git status
```

git add : To add files to staging area

```
    git add <filename>

    git add .
```

git restore : To unstage files + discard file changes

# Unstage the file

git restore --staged <file-name>

# Discard changes in working tree

git restore <file-name>

Note:

Be careful with:

```
    git restore <file-name>
```

because uncommitted changes can be discarded.

git commit : Send files from staging area to local repository

```
    git commit -m "commit message"
```

Example:

```
    git commit -m "Add user service configuration"
```

git log : To check commit history

```
    git log
```

# Compact commit history

git log --oneline

# Show recent commits

git log --oneline -10

git push : Send commits from local repository to remote repository

```
    git push
```

git clone : Download remote repository to local machine

```
    git clone <repo-url>
```

Example:

```
    git clone https://github.com/<username>/<repo>.git
```

git pull : Download latest changes from remote repository
and integrate them into current branch

```
    git pull
```

git rm : Remove files from Git and working tree

```
    git rm <file-name>
```

Note:

After git rm, the deletion becomes staged.

To publish the deletion to remote repository:

```
    git commit -m "Remove unused file"

    git push
```

