=============
Git Branches
============

=> Multiple teams may work on the same project in parallel

```
    - Ongoing development
    - Bug fixing
    - POC / R&D
    - Production support
    - Release preparation
    - Hotfixes
```

=> If every team directly changes the same branch,
collaboration becomes difficult.

******* To overcome this problem we use Git branches *********

=> Git branches allow developers to work independently
and safely in parallel.

=> Using Git branches:

```
    Team-A can work on Feature-A

    Team-B can work on Feature-B

    Team-C can work on Bug-Fix

    Release Team can prepare release
```

=> Example enterprise branches:

```
    - main
    - develop
    - feature/*
    - bugfix/*
    - release/*
    - hotfix/*
```

Note:

Branch names are decided according to the organization's
branching strategy.

We can create multiple branches in a Git repository.

Note:

When we clone a Git repository, Git normally checks out
the repository's default branch.

# Clone repository

git clone <repo-url>

# Clone a specific branch

git clone -b <branch-name> <repo-url>

# Display current branch

git branch

# Display all local branches

git branch

# Display local + remote branches

git branch -a

# Create new branch

git branch <branch-name>

# Switch branch

git switch <branch-name>

# Create and switch to new branch

git switch -c <branch-name>

Note:

`git switch` is the modern command for branch switching.

Older projects may still use:

```
    git checkout <branch-name>
```

# Delete local branch

git branch -d <branch-name>

# Force delete local branch

git branch -D <branch-name>

